GL renderer: release-build cost counters in the heartbeat ("gl").

At 60 FPS the one measurement of internal resolution 5x on record is a host
failure: 2,580 presents against 3,823 emulated VBlanks, while 2x was clean.
What could not be answered from the build the player runs is WHY - frame_perf,
which splits a frame into scene/present GPU time, only exists in the
debug-tools build behind the TCP server. The candidates all grow with the
internal scale S, or turn GPU time into emulation-thread time:

  - SwapWindow blocking because the driver's queue is full (GPU-bound)
  - ensure_cpu's synchronous glReadPixels for guest VRAM reads, which waits
    for everything the GPU has queued
  - full-surface work: the mask stencil rebuilt from alpha over the whole
    1024S x 512S surface and every native-wide surface, and the hold-last
    copy of the whole drawable on every present
  - batch splits, semi-transparent prims drawn one per batch, and native-wide
    mirror passes

Each is now a monotonic total in a GlCost struct, bumped on the render thread
and written by the heartbeat thread as a "gl" JSON object
(gl_renderer_cost_json): presents, swap_us, batches, semi_isolated, the seven
existing batch-break reasons, stencil_rebuilds/_px, sync_reads/_skips/_us,
hold_copies/_px, pack_px, mirror_passes. The launcher and
tuning/scripts/scale_bench.py take rates over a window, like the cadence
counters.

GPU timings: PSX_GPU_PERF=1 turns on the existing GL_TIME_ELAPSED brackets in
a release build (they were compiled out with the debug server), without the
native-wide mirror timestamps, which are hundreds of queries a frame and stay
debug-only. Totals gpu_frames / gpu_scene_us / gpu_present_us, and
gpu_scene_max_us for the worst frame of the last second. Debug-tools builds
behave as before (on unless PSX_GL_PERF=0).

No file here is in codegen_hash_sources.cmake; overlay shards are unaffected.

